## 1. Task overview

**Task mode:** {{TASK_MODE}}
<!-- TASK_MODE: one of {classification, extraction}. The single source
     of truth for the task type (DESIGN.md §7.1.11). Filled by the
     designer during /spp-init, after the strawman and before the
     schema-designer sub-skill runs, because the mode governs which
     OUTPUT_SCHEMA shapes (§2) and which metrics (§4) are in play.
       - classification: each row resolves to a fixed output space — one
         categorical label, or a fixed set of fields each with its own
         label space. Scored by F1 / balanced-accuracy / per-field
         metrics.
       - extraction: each row resolves to a variable-cardinality,
         possibly span-grounded set of items (named entities, spans,
         redaction targets). Scored by alignment-based set P/R/F1,
         span-IoU / span-F1, or deterministic leakage.
     TASK_MODE and OUTPUT_SCHEMA (§2) must agree; the schema-designer
     sub-skill rejects a contradiction (e.g. extraction mode with a bare
     enum schema). Absent or unset reads as classification, so every
     pre-v0.10 plan is unchanged. -->

**One-sentence description:** {{TASK_DESCRIPTION}}
<!-- TASK_DESCRIPTION: what this prompt classifies or extracts, in plain
     English. E.g. "classify customer support tickets as billing-related
     or not", or "extract every product name mentioned in a review."
     Filled by user during /spp-init consultation. -->

**Audience for the prompt's output:** {{AUDIENCE}}
<!-- AUDIENCE: who or what consumes the classifications downstream.
     E.g. "internal triage queue", "alerting pipeline", "human review
     team." Affects metric choice (precision vs recall) downstream. -->

**Problem statement** (2–3 sentences on why this task matters and
what currently goes wrong without it):
{{PROBLEM_STATEMENT}}
<!-- PROBLEM_STATEMENT: filled by user during /spp-init. The designer
     should push back if the answer is fewer than two sentences or
     conflates the task with the metric. -->

